=== Taxonomy ===

==== Taxonomic history ====

In 1754, [[Philip Miller]] set up the genus ''Abies''; he also defined the type species ''[[Abies alba]]''.<ref name="Farjon Rushworth 1989"/><ref>{{cite book |last=Miller |first=Philip |author-link=Philip Miller |year=1754 |title=The Gardener's Dictionary |edition=4th, abridged |volume=1}}</ref> In 1878, [[George Engelmann]] classified only a part of the genus;<ref name="Farjon Rushworth 1989"/> [[Heinrich Mayr]] did the same in 1890,<ref name="Farjon Rushworth 1989"/><ref>{{cite book |last=Mayr |first=Heinrich |year=1890 |title=Monographie der Abielineen des Japanischen Reiches
|location=Munchen}}</ref> as did the German botanist Wilhelm Patschke in 1913.<ref name="Farjon Rushworth 1989"/> The classifications by Paul Robert Hickel in 1906 to 1908,<ref name="Farjon Rushworth 1989"/><ref>Hickel, Robert (1906-08). Notes pour servir ä la determination pratique des Abietinees. Bull. Soc. Dendrol. France 2: 45 58, f. I 7(15 xi 1906); 3; 5 18, f. 8 30 (15 ii 1907); 4: 41 48, f. 31 46(15v 1907); 5: 82 86(15 viii 1907); 7:5-10, f. a 1 (15 ii 1908); 9; 179-185 (15 viii 1908); 10: 201 208 (15 xi 1908).</ref> and by P. Landry in 1984,<ref name="Farjon Rushworth 1989"/><ref name="Landry 1984">{{cite journal |last=Landry |first=Pierre |title=Synopsis du genre Abies |journal=Bulletin de la Société Botanique de France. Lettres Botaniques |volume=131 |issue=3 |date=1984 |doi=10.1080/01811797.1984.10824633 |pages=223–229 |bibcode=1984BSBFL.131..223L |url=http://www.tandfonline.com/doi/abs/10.1080/01811797.1984.10824633 |language=fr|url-access=subscription }}</ref> made use only of a subset of the available morphological characteristics.<ref name="Farjon Rushworth 1989">{{cite journal |last1=Farjon |first1=Aljos |author1-link=Aljos Farjon |last2=Rushworth |first2=K. D. |title=A Classification of Abies Miller (Pinaceae) |journal= Notes from the Royal Botanic Garden Edinburgh |date=1989 |volume=46 |issue=1 |pages=59–79 |doi=10.24823/nrbge.1989.3565 |doi-access=free |url=https://repository.naturalis.nl/pub/535254/MPUUH1988003001007.pdf}}</ref> In 1990 and 2001, the Dutch botanist [[Aljos Farjon]] attempted a more complete classification; he accepted 48 species within the genus; in 2017 he reduced this to 46. Adopting a different approach, in 2011 Z. Debreczy and I. Rácz treated the genus as containing 67 species.<ref name="IDS">{{cite web |title=Abies Mill. |url=https://www.treesandshrubsonline.org/articles/abies/ |publisher=International Dendrology Society |access-date=8 September 2025}}</ref>

==== Species ====

{{As of|2025|September}}, [[Plants of the World Online]] accepted 49 species.<ref>{{cite web |title=Abies Mill. Accepted Species<!--tab on page--> |url=https://powo.science.kew.org/taxon/urn:lsid:ipni.org:names:325655-2#children |publisher=[[Royal Botanic Gardens, Kew]] |access-date=10 September 2025}}</ref> The sections are based on Stull et al. 2021.<ref name="Stull Qu 2021"/><!--see also https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ympev.2018.03.009 Semerikova 2018-->

Section ''Abies'' is found in central, south, and eastern Europe and Asia Minor.

* ''[[Abies alba]]'' – silver fir or European silver fir
* ''[[Abies nebrodensis]]'' – Sicilian fir
* ''[[Abies borisii-regis]]'' – Bulgarian fir
* ''[[Abies cephalonica]]'' – Greek fir
* ''[[Abies nordmanniana]]'' – Caucasian fir or Nordmann fir
** [[Abies nordmanniana subsp. equi-trojani|''Abies nordmanniana'' subsp. ''equi-trojani'']] – Kazdağı fir, Turkish fir
* ''[[Abies pinsapo]]'' – Spanish fir
** ''Abies pinsapo'' var. ''marocana'' – Moroccan fir
* ''[[Abies numidica]]'' – Algerian fir
* ''[[Abies cilicica]]'' – Syrian fir

Section ''Balsamea'' is found in northern Asia and North America, and high mountains further south.

* ''[[Abies fraseri]]'' – Fraser's fir
* ''[[Abies balsamea]]'' – balsam fir
** ''Abies balsamea'' var. ''phanerolepis'' – bracted balsam fir
